===Etymology===
Modern {{uder|en|el|σπανακόπιτα}}, from {{m|el|σπανάκι||spinach}} + {{m|el|πίτα||pie}}. Usually short for {{m|el|σπανακοτυρόπιτα||spanakotiropita, spinach and cheese pie}}, from same as above and {{m|el|τυρί||cheese}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌspænəˈkoʊpɪtə/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-spanakopita.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|+|spanakopites}}

# (Usually short for [[spanakotiropita]]) A Greek dish made with pre-cooked [[spinach]], butter, olive oil, cheese, [[green onion]]s, [[egg]], and seasoning in [[phyllo pastry]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author=w:Hilma Wolitzer|chapter=30|title=In the Palomar Arms|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Farrar, Straus and Giroux|Farrar/Straus/Giroux]]|page=231|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/inpalomararms00woli/page/231/mode/1up|isbn=9780374176563|passage=Soon, she’s toting a tray of miniature '''spanakopites''', wedging it between groups of men and women who all seem to have arrived at once, as if they’ve been brought in a chartered bus to populate the house, to form a small privileged nation.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Olympia Rizidis|chapter=Olga Returns to Her Parents|title=This Is Our Promised Land|location=Brisbane, Qld.|publisher=w:Boolarong Press|page=42|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Cz2yaPcDcasC&pg=PA42|isbn=9781921920318|passage=They laughed and enjoyed baking bread, buns and '''spanakopites''' (her Mama taught her the art of making filo pastry).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=w:Lynn Freed|title=The Last Laugh: A Novel|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Farrar, Straus and Giroux|Sarah Crichton Books]]|page=94|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/lastlaughnovel0000free_r7f3/page/94/mode/1up|isbn=9780374286651|passage=Bowls of melitzanosalata and tzatziki had been laid out, platters of '''spanakopita''', meatballs, tiropitas, and two bottles of wine.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Mary Karras|chapter=London – 1986|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cAsWEAAAQBAJ&pg=PT204|title=The Making of Mrs Petrakis|location=London|publisher=w:Two Roads|isbn=978 1 529 34558 2|passage=A large order today, he’d said. Mrs Yiasoumi had a delicate stomach and could hardly get out of bed, so he’d take five of her freshest '''spanakopites'''.}}
# The same or similar dishes made without cheese.

